.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm \- manage Euclid S3-compatible storage buckets and objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B esm
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B esm
module talks to the Euclid gateway's esm service to create and manage
S3-compatible storage buckets and the objects stored in them, and to
subscribe EQS queues or ENS topics to receive object-created
notifications. Every
.B esm
command sends a single JSON request to the gateway
(\fB\-\-endpoint\fR, default \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR) and prints the
JSON response on success, with a few exceptions:
.B upload-file
streams the file's raw bytes to the gateway as part of a multipart
upload,
.B download-file
receives an object's raw bytes back the same way as part of a
multipart download, and
.BR upload-directory " and " download-bucket
do this once per file (or object) under a local directory (or bucket
prefix), printing a JSON array (one entry per file) instead of a
single response.
.PP
Everything that changes an object also publishes an event
.RI ( esm.object.created ,
.I esm.object.updated
or
.IR esm.object.deleted ),
which an application can subscribe to with
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
rather than polling a listing for changes. That is a different mechanism
from
.B subscribe
below: this module's subscriptions deliver a notification into an EQS
queue or an ENS topic you created, while
.B ees
hands the event straight to a named consumer and keeps it until that
consumer acknowledges it.
.PP
All
.B esm
commands require the caller to be authenticated; see
.B AUTHENTICATION
below for how the session used to sign these requests is obtained.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-bucket
Creates a new storage bucket. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1).
.TP
.B list-buckets
Lists buckets, optionally filtered by name prefix, paged and sorted. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-buckets (1).
.TP
.B get-bucket-ern
Resolves a bucket's Euclid resource name (ERN) by its name. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-ern (1).
.TP
.B get-bucket-size
Returns a bucket's total size in bytes. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-size (1).
.TP
.B delete-bucket
Deletes a bucket by its ERN. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-bucket (1).
.TP
.B rename-bucket
Gives a bucket another name, rewriting its ERN and every object and
subscription that names it. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-rename-bucket (1).
.TP
.B enable-encryption
Encrypts the objects written to a bucket from now on, under an EKM key
named by the command or created for it. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-enable-encryption (1).
.TP
.B disable-encryption
Stops encrypting the objects written to a bucket. Applies to new
uploads and puts only \- what is already stored stays encrypted and its
key is untouched. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-disable-encryption (1).
.TP
.B upload-file
Uploads a local file to a bucket, transparently as a multipart upload.
See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1).
.TP
.B upload-directory
Uploads every file in a local directory to a bucket, one object per
file. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-directory (1).
.TP
.B download-file
Downloads an object from a bucket to a local file, transparently as a
multipart download. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1).
.TP
.B download-bucket
Downloads a bucket's objects to a local directory, one file per
object. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-bucket (1).
.TP
.B list-objects
Lists a bucket's objects, optionally filtered by name prefix, paged and
sorted. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1).
.TP
.B get-object-count
Returns the number of objects in a bucket, optionally filtered by
object key prefix. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-object-count (1).
.TP
.B delete-object
Deletes an object by its ERN. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1).
.TP
.B add-object-attribute
Adds a user-defined attribute to an object, which must not have one of
that name yet. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-add-object-attribute (1).
.TP
.B set-object-attribute
Changes the value of an attribute an object already carries. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-set-object-attribute (1).
.TP
.B list-object-attributes
Lists every attribute of an object, with each value's type. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-object-attributes (1).
.TP
.B delete-object-attribute
Removes one attribute from an object. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object-attribute (1).
.TP
.B copy-object
Copies an object to another key or bucket, bytes and all. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-copy-object (1).
.TP
.B move-object
Moves an object to another key or bucket, without copying its bytes. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-move-object (1).
.TP
.B rename-object
Renames an object within its bucket. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-rename-object (1).
.TP
.B purge-bucket
Removes all objects from a bucket, leaving the bucket itself in place.
See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1).
.TP
.B subscribe
Subscribes a target resource (an EQS queue or an ENS topic) to a
bucket's object-created events. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-subscribe (1).
.TP
.B unsubscribe
Deletes a subscription. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-unsubscribe (1).
.TP
.B list-subscriptions
Lists the subscriptions of a bucket. See
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-subscriptions (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I esm
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a JSON configuration file providing defaults for action
options - currently
.I euclid.modules.esm.part-size
and
.I euclid.modules.esm.concurrency
, used by
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1)
and
.BR euclid-cli-esm-download-file (1).
Defaults to
.IR /usr/local/euclid/etc/euclid.json ;
silently ignored if the file doesn't exist, so
.B esm
works with no config file present.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIsigv4\fR (the
default) or \fIrfc9421\fR for RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures. Both
prove the request with the same access key; RFC 9421 puts the
signature in \fISignature\fR/\fISignature\-Input\fR headers and binds
the body through \fIContent\-Digest\fR (RFC 9530) instead of using
AWS's Authorization header. Overrides
.I euclid.cli.signature
from the configuration file. Ignored for
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
commands, which authenticate with the session's bearer token.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B esm
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
Unlike
.B eam
commands, which always send the bearer token,
.B esm
requests are signed with SigV4 (the same scheme Euclid SDKs use for S3)
using the access key ID and secret stored in that file, if present;
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
provisions this access key automatically on first login, so a separate
.B eam create-access-key
call is not normally needed. If no access key is stored, the bearer
token is sent instead as an
.I "Authorization: Bearer"
header.
.PP
If the server rejects a request with
.IR "HTTP 401" ,
the stored credentials are left untouched (unlike
.BR eam ,
which clears them on 401); run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again if requests start failing with 401.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it (missing/expired session, invalid input, bucket or object
not found, etc.). Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or SigV4
access key, created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B esm
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a bucket, upload a file to it, and list its objects:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm create-bucket \e
    --name reports

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm upload-file \e
    --bucket-ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports \e
    --key 2026-08.csv --file ./2026-08.csv

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm list-objects \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Download that file back down under a different name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm download-file \e
    --bucket-ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports \e
    --key 2026-08.csv --file ./2026-08-restored.csv
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check a bucket's size, then delete an object and the bucket:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm get-bucket-size \e
    --ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm delete-object \e
    --ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:object:2026-08.csv

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm delete-bucket \e
    --ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Empty a bucket without deleting it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm purge-bucket \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
Subscribe a queue to a bucket's object-created events, then remove the
subscription:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm subscribe \e
    --source-ern ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports \e
    --target-ern ern:eqs:eu-central-1:000000000000:queue:upload-events

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm list-subscriptions \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:bucket:reports

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 esm unsubscribe \e
    --subscription ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:subscription:6f1b1e2e...
.fi
.RE
